    <section class="section">
      <h2>Abstract</h2>
      <p>
       Charlotte Brontë’s final, desolate novel <i>Villette</i> (1853), often named as her “mature” masterpiece, is a narrative of exile and displacement: one woman’s flight from her home in England to a radically uncertain future in a foreign city. A distinctly phenomenological novelist, in <i>Villette</i> Brontë maps the affective crises of exile and aloneness onto the transmogrifications of sensory life. In this paper, I address Brontë’s ambivalent <i>Bildung</i> and its complex disavowal of orthodox novelistic form. Avowedly ambivalent about her progress in the world, protagonist Lucy Snowe shrewdly repudiates material and worldly success, acting even with nonchalance about her very survival. At the same time, the heroine’s lovelessness is felt as a crisis so acute that it threatens the teleological drive of realist plotting, undermining any possibility of characterological progress —- at least according to the genre’s well-established conventions. In this way, Brontë emphasises both the temptations and perils of conforming to the conventional plot of female development, eschewing marriage for her heroine in preference for an enigmatic and haunting conclusion. I shall ask whether there can be a <i>bildungsroman</i> of regression, an alternative to the two conventional fates of the Victorian female character: between economic, social, and romantic success, on the one hand, and death or ignominy, on the other?</p

<p>Drawing on the phenomenology of Maurice Merleau-Ponty, I will read Villette as a novel that thematizes its own hauntedness and dis-ease, of painful non-coincidence, addressing what Terry Castle has described as the “ghostification” of the psyche; for if the Victorian realist text tropes on the uncanniness of thought itself, then Villette must surely be its paradigmatic expression.  </p>

    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Bio</h2>
      <p>
       Abe Roth is a Professor in the Philosophy Department at Ohio State University.  He received a Ph.D. from Princeton in 1996, and was on the faculty in Philosophy at UCLA from 1998-2002, and at the University of Illinois at Chicago from 2002-2006. He works mainly in the philosophy of action (especially shared agency and collective intentionality), and related areas of epistemology, mind, moral psychology, ethics, and social ontology.  He has published in various places, including <i>The Philosophical Review</i>, <i>Noûs</i>, <i>Ethics</i>, <i>Oxford Studies in Agency and Responsibility</i>, <i>Philosophy and Phenomenological Research</i>, and <i>Philosophical Studies</i>.  
      </p>
